      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{595459a74f08c21baf12e507|full_name}: It would work just like deleting the node, yes. The flow would stop, and the connected noodles would all be disabled and not functioning too. But importantly, these noodles are not disconnected. The point is to preserve node placement, and all of its connections while also being disabled. It's almost like you can \"Think of it as a way of commenting out a node while leaving it in your code structure..\"\n\n> simply removing the flow noodle accomplishes the same thing as disabling a node, doesn't it?\n\n\"Simply\" is an assumption. I think this sort of node disabling feature could be useful for situations where the connections are many, or messy, and you would rather comment (disable) that line or lines of code (node(s)) quick. - - (Not delete them and then have to keep in mind exactly all the things it was connected to and where they are in your un-zoomable graph.)\n\nIf you delete code and retype it while debugging, more power to you. I don't like to do that. I'd rather comment it out temporarily.\n\nAs I said in the OP (and just now added \"optionally\" to), a more complicated version of the \"disable a node to 'comment' it out\" would enable you to optionally allow flow, but other functions of the node would not execute or respond to noodles.\n\nComments in normal code don't necessarily halt code execution order the first time the compiler hits a comment. So this would optionally make it work like that, _flow wise_. But as I said above, I don't know if that is crazy. See: overly complicated.\n\nFor anyone still confused. This is requested ONLY for debugging purposes.\n\nI used the term \"commenting\" because that is common terminology used for disabling code while not removing it in a program. Real Graph Comments are obviously different. They are not part of this.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-12-18T04:01:53.844Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Think of it as a way of commenting out a node while leaving it in your code structure.\n\nUseful for debugging. Like in the case where you don't want to reconnect a bunch of noodles to check if taking the node out fixes your bug.\n\nOr if you are one to horde commented out code you will appreciate it too.\n\nA more complicated version of the feature would _optionally_ allow commenting out the node, but allowing flow to continue. But, I dunno if that is just crazy.",
